# Read by GitHub, which turns it into the "Cite this repository" button, and by
# cff-convert, Zenodo and Zotero. It is a claim about authorship and about what the
# software does, so it is held to the same rule as every other claim here: nothing in
# it is asserted that a reader cannot check in this repository.
#
# Two fields are deliberately absent until the first tagged release:
#   version         - there is no tag yet, and a version nobody can check out is worse
#                     than none. Add it in the same commit that cuts the tag.
#   date-released   - same reason. CFF wants YYYY-MM-DD and it must be the tag's date.
#   doi / identifiers - a DOI is what makes this citable in a paper rather than in a
#                     blog post. Zenodo mints one per GitHub release once the
#                     repository is connected to it; that is a per-repository switch
#                     and it has not been thrown. Until it is, the citation below is
#                     a software citation with a URL and no DOI, which is valid CFF
#                     and is what most tooling will render.
#
# `make check` does not validate this file. `pip install cffconvert` and
# `cffconvert --validate` is the check, and it is worth running after any edit -
# an invalid CITATION.cff makes GitHub drop the button silently rather than
# complain.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this harness, or quote a figure taken from data/runs/, please cite it
  as below. Quoting a rate without the run it came from is the one use this
  repository is written to make harder.
title: >-
  proxy-benchmark: a provider-agnostic harness for measuring where a target refuses
  a client
abstract: >-
  A measurement harness for proxy providers, browser engines and scraping targets.
  It reports at which layer a request is rejected - IP reputation, browser signals,
  or the TLS handshake - and what passing that layer costs in traffic and time.
  Every verdict is read from the response body rather than from the HTTP status,
  because a refusal is routinely served as 200 with a stub body. Every attempt is
  written as one JSONL row carrying its full parameter set, and the runs are
  committed, so a published figure can be recomputed offline by a reader with no
  proxy account. The runner is never told which provider it is talking to: it takes
  a gateway host, port and username template from configuration, so measuring a
  competitor, or a proxy the reader already owns, is a credentials change rather
  than a code change. Results are published including the settings under which the
  authors' own product performs worst.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Kazmin
    given-names: Alexandr
    email: alexandr.kazmin@nodemaven.com
    affiliation: NodeMaven
    alias: aleekaz
repository-code: "https://github.com/nodemaven/proxy-benchmark"
url: "https://github.com/nodemaven/proxy-benchmark"
license: MIT
keywords:
  - web-scraping
  - proxy
  - residential-proxy
  - anti-bot
  - bot-detection
  - browser-fingerprinting
  - tls-fingerprinting
  - benchmark
  - reproducibility
  - measurement
